## Technologies Used

* HTML
* CSS
* JavaScript (localStorage, DOM manipulation)

## Challenges

One major challenge was managing navigation between 
pages, especially when using the back button. The 
browser history sometimes redirected users to 
unexpected pages. 

Another challenge was implementing the favorites 
system using localStorage and ensuring data consistency
across pages.

## What I Learned

* How to use localStorage to store user data
1. How to store recipe cards into favorites

// Retrieve existing favorites from localStorage
// If no data exists yet, initialize an empty array
let favorites = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("favorites")) || [];


// Function to add a recipe item into favorites
function addToFavorites(item){

// Check if the item already exists in favorites
// This prevents duplicate entries
let exists = favorites.some(fav => fav.name === item.name);

if(!exists){
// Add the new item into the array
favorites.push(item);

// Convert the array into a string and store it in localStorage
localStorage.setItem("favorites", JSON.stringify(favorites));

    alert("Added to favorites!");
  } else {
    alert("Already in favorites!");
  }
}

<!-- Button connected to each recipe card -->
<!-- When clicked, it sends an object containing recipe data -->
<button onclick="addToFavorites({
  name: 'Basque Cheesecake',
  page: 'basque.html',
  image: 'images/basque.jpg'
})">
  Add to Favorites
</button>

--> How to store arrays in local storage?
// The structure of the data I am storing:
// An array of objects, where each object represents a recipe
favorites = [
  {
    name: "Basque Cheesecake",
    page: "basque.html",
    image: "images/basque.jpg"
  }
]

/// When storing the data:
// localStorage only supports strings,
// so I convert the array into a JSON string
localStorage.setItem("favorites", JSON.stringify(favorites));


